column8:
Section 6(1) of CARO requires FIs and DNFBPs to obtain and verify, with respect to individuals, legal persons, and legal arrangements, as follows:
- For individuals: full name and address, identity card number or details of any official document of identity, date and place of birth, occupation status, name of employer or nature of self-employment or business, contact number, sufficient information to understand the purpose and intended nature of the business relationship and its ownership and control structure;
- For legal persons: corporate name, certified true copy of charter, articles of incorporation or other official documents, registered office address or principal place of business, laws that regulate and bind the legal person, sufficient information to understand the purpose and intended nature of the business relationship, names of directors and senior management, identity of all natural persons who ultimately has a controlling ownership interest or exercise control;
- For legal arrangements: name of the arrangement, identity of the settler, trustee or protector, beneficiaries and any other natural persons having effective control over the trust, certified copy of the declaration of trust or other official document confirming the continued existence of the arrangement, registered office address or principal place of business, laws that regulate and bind the legal arrangement, sufficient information to understand the purpose and intended nature of the business relationship.

Section 6(4) allows FIs and DNFPBs to adopt simplified measures for due diligence, provided that the simplified measures are made in writing and the Authority has given prior approval for its implementation.

column9:
Pursuant to Section 9, FIs and DNFBPs are required to conduct EDD on the following PEPs:
1. Domestic PEPs whose activities may pose a high risk of ML/TF.
2. Foreign PEPs.

EDD measures include:
- to obtain any additional information on the customers and beneficial owners other than those set out in section 6;
- to enquire on the source of wealth or source of funds;
- to obtain approval from the senior management of the financial institution or designated non-financial business and profession before it establishes or continues its business relations with its customers;
- to increase ongoing monitoring of the activities of its customer or the beneficial owner and its business relations and transactions with the customer or the beneficial owner to prevent money laundering and financing of terrorism or the commission of any other serious offences.

column10:
Section 10 requires FIs to:
- identify and verify the identification of respondent institutions with which they conduct correspondent banking relationships.
- collect information to enable it to fully understand on the nature of the respondent institution's activities;
- based on publicly available information, evaluate the respondent institution's reputation and the nature of supervision to which it is subject including whether it has been subject to a ML/TF investigation or regulatory action;
- obtain approval from senior management before establishing a correspondent banking relationship;
- evaluate the controls implemented by the respondent institution with respect to anti-money laundering and combating the financing of terrorism (AML/CFT);
- establish an agreement on the respective responsibilities of each party under the relationship;
- in the case of a payable through account, ensure that the respondent institution has carried out CDD on its customers that have direct access to the accounts of the correspondent, has implemented mechanisms for ongoing monitoring with respect to its customers and is capable of providing relevant customer due diligence information on request;
- not enter into or continue business relations with a shell bank; and 
- not to enter into or continue business relations with a respondent financial institution that permits its accounts to be used by a shell bank.

column11:
Pursuant to Section 5(3), FIs and DNFBPs may rely on intermediaries or other third parties for CDD if:
- there is no suspicion of money laundering or financing of terrorism;
- information on the identity of each customer and beneficial owner is provided immediately on opening of the account or commencement of the business relationship;
- the third party is able to provide without delay copies of identification informtion and other documents relating to the obligation of due diligence upon request and is established in or is subject to the jurisdiction of a country where such person is subject to requirements consistent with standards set by the FATF and has adequate measures in place to comply with those requirements
- the FI has internal policies and procedures in place to mitigate risks of ML/TF.
- there is a written arrangements governing the relationship between the FI and the third party, specifying the rights, responsibilities and expectations of both parties.

column17:
Pursuant to Section 22(1), FIs and DNFBPs shall develop and implement programmes for the prevention of money laundering and financing of terrorism (ML/TF) which have regard to the ML/TF risks and the size of the business. Such programmes shall include:
- internal policies, procedures and controls to ensure compliance with AML/CFT laws and regulations;
- adequate screening procedures to ensure high standards when hiring employees;
- ongoing training for officers and employees;
- policies and procedures to identify and assess the risks of ML/TF resulting from the development of new products and business practices;
- policies and procedures to identify, assess and understand AML/CFT risks posed by customers and beneficial owners, including if they are politically-exposed persons, the countries where they are from or the countries where they have operations, and their products and services; and
- independent audit arrangements

Pursuant to Section 22(2), FIs and DNFBPs shall designate 
a compliance officer at management level to be responsible for the implementation of, and ongoing compliance with, this Order by the institution. Such compliance officer shall have ready access to all books, records and employees of the financial institution or designated non-financial business and profession necessary to fulfill his responsibilities

column20:
Targeted Financial Sanctions are supported under CARO through a framework that enables the implementation through:
- suspicion-based reporting;
- investigative powers;
- freezing, seizure and forfeiture of property; and
- FIU cooperation domestically and internationally.

