column12:
-Paragraph 4.3.1:  requirement to keep customer information updated;
-Paragraph 4.3.2: ongoing monitoring of customer transactions;
-Paragraph 4.4: Reinforces the need for maintaining updated customer information for continuous assessment of activity;

column14:
-Paragraph 1.2 establishes a mandatory obligation to submit STRs;
-Paragraph 4.1 states the requirement to report suspicious transactions with no monetary threshold;  
-Paragraph 5.1 prescribed timeframe for STR submission;
-Paragraph 5.2 states the requirement to accelerate reporting for TF related STRs;
-Paragraph 6.2 states the mandatory prohibition against tipping off in relation to STRs;
-Paragraph 7.1 System for STR submission (IFIS);
-Paragraphs 8.1-8.2: Minimum information and content requirements for STR submission;
-Paragraph 8.2.6: Requirement to include supporting documentation with STR;

column17:
-Paragraph 4.3.2 states the requirement to have internal monitoring systems or controls to detect unusual or suspicious transactions;
-Paragraphs 5.1-5.3 states the requirements to designate responsibility within the organisation, reflecting internal control and governance arrangements for STR reporting;
-Paragraph 5.3.2 states Clear allocation of internal responsibility and escalation process, forming part of internal control frameworks;
-Paragraph 6.3 states requirement for access controls and confidentiality safeguards

Relevant sections in the Guidelines:
- Board and senior management accountability: paragraph 6.1;
- Formalised policies and procedures: paragraphs 6.1 and 6.4;
- System-based monitoring controls: Section 5 and paragraph 6.2;
- Independent compliance testing: paragraph 6.5;
- Training and competency: paragraph 6.6; and
- Record keeping: paragraph 6.3.

column7:
EKYC Notice:-
- Paragraph 1.2 States that the Notice outlines minimum required standards for “customer due diligence measures” for non‑face‑to‑face relationships;
- paragraph 3.3 Requires compliance with CARO obligations on obtaining and verifying customer information using reliable sources, explicitly applying statutory CDD requirements to E‑KYC;
- paragraph 4.1 Prescribes identification and verification steps during onboarding pursuant to CARO sections 6 and 7, explicitly detailing the conduct of CDD using E‑KYC;
- paragraphs 4.3-4.5 Allows use of E‑KYC for periodic review, updating customer information, and re‑verification where new documents are required, explicitly addressing ongoing CDD processes.

EKYC Guidelines:-
- Paragraph 1.3.1 States that the Guidelines aim to ensure “Requirements for Anti‑Money Laundering and Combating the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) relating to customer onboarding and ongoing customer due diligence are adhered to”, explicitly linking E‑KYC measures to conduct of CDD.
- requirements for identity verification undertaken during CDD (paragraphs 3.1-3.4)
- Paragraph 4.1 describing CDD identification and verification measures;
- Paragraph 4.2 requires E‑KYC to detect customer records and link to customer databases to support “complete KYC/CDD requirements and risk categorisation”;
- paragraph 5.1 allows E‑KYC to be used for periodic reviews of customer information, explicitly covering ongoing CDD.

column8:
EKYC Notice:-
- Paragraph 1.2 States that the Notice outlines minimum required standards for “customer due diligence measures” for non‑face‑to‑face relationships;
- paragraph 3.3 Requires compliance with CARO obligations on obtaining and verifying customer information using reliable sources, explicitly applying statutory CDD requirements to E‑KYC;
- paragraph 4.1 Prescribes identification and verification steps during onboarding pursuant to CARO sections 6 and 7, explicitly detailing the conduct of CDD using E‑KYC;
- paragraphs 4.3-4.5 Allows use of E‑KYC for periodic review, updating customer information, and re‑verification where new documents are required, explicitly addressing ongoing CDD processes.

EKYC Guidelines:-
- Paragraph 1.3.1 States that the Guidelines aim to ensure “Requirements for Anti‑Money Laundering and Combating the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) relating to customer onboarding and ongoing customer due diligence are adhered to”, explicitly linking E‑KYC measures to conduct of CDD.
- requirements for identity verification undertaken during CDD (paragraphs 3.1-3.4)
- Paragraph 4.1 describing CDD identification and verification measures;
- Paragraph 4.2 requires E‑KYC to detect customer records and link to customer databases to support “complete KYC/CDD requirements and risk categorisation”;
- paragraph 5.1 allows E‑KYC to be used for periodic reviews of customer information, explicitly covering ongoing CDD.

column11:
EKYC Notice:-
- Paragraph 5.12 requires that for “any third-party E-KYC application, the vendor or service provider shall demonstrate the acceptable FAR and provide sufficient test and audit reports”, explicitly covering reliance on third‑party systems for CDD-related verification;
- Paragraph 6.4 requires that where an E‑KYC solution is “derived or outsourced from a third party”, Banks and Financial Institutions must demonstrate compliance with “intermediaries or third party conditions as per section 5(3) of CARO”, explicitly governing third‑party reliance for CDD

EKYC Guidelines:-
- Paragraph 6.8 states that E‑KYC data “should not be shared to other third-party unless consented by the customer”, explicitly addressing conditions under which third parties may be involved in handling CDD-related data;
- Paragraph 6.12 specifies that if the E‑KYC solution “is hosted or provided by a third-party”, institutions must observe IT Third Party Risk Management Guidelines, explicitly acknowledging third‑party involvement in E‑KYC supporting CDD

column13:
EKYC Notice:-
- explicit requirements for compliance with record-keeping requirements under section 14 CARO (paragraph 4.2);
- requirements for all successful and failed authentications shall be recorded (paragraph 5.11);
- requirements to keep record of EKYC data and EKYC system performance to support review and compliance, an explicit record retention requirement (paragraph 5.14).

EKYC Guidelines:-
- In compliance with record keeping requirements, Banks and FIs should ensure the EKYC is able to detect any records or information on the customer and be accessible or linked to the customer database so as to assist Banks and FIs in conducting complete KYC/CD requirements and risk categorisation of its customers (paragraph 4.2);
- storage/retention and use limitations (paragraph 6.8);
- requirements on the retention of logs and audit records for review and anlysis (paragraph 6.9).

column18:
EKYC Notice:-
- Identification and analysis of ML/TF and EKYC risks (Paragraph 6.1);
- Risk-based prohibition for high-risk customers (Paragraph 6.2):
- Risk-based customer categorisation (Paragraph 4.3);
- Assessment and control of identification error risk (Paragraphs 5.8-5.10) 

EKYC Guidelines:-
- Periodic assessment of effectiveness and bias (paragraph 6.10);
- Security and vulnerability assessment (paragraph 6.11);
- Assurance over risk management (paragraph 7.1)

'Risk assessment is required to determine suitability of non‑face‑to‑face onboarding methods

column8:
-Mandatory conduct of CDD before establishing a business relationship (paragraph 2.1);
- Timing requirement for completing CDD (onboarding) (paragraph 2.2);
- requirements to identify purpose and intended nature of the relationship, identify source of funds and source of wealth for PEP customers and determining the types and volumes of transactions (paragraphs 2.2.1-2.2.3);
- Ongoing obligation to keep CDD information accurate and up to date (paragraph 2.3);
- Requirement to risk-classify customers as part of risk-based CDD (paragraph 2.4)

column12:
- Paragraph 2.3 states the requirement to keep customer information updated after onboarding;
- Paragraph 2.4 requires continous customer risk categorisation, supporting ongoing due diligence;
- Paragraph 4.1 uses existing customer profiles and transaction history as part of continous monitoring;
- Paragraph 4.4 requires FIs and DNFBPs to periodically update customer risk profiles based on monitoring outcomes.

column14:
- Paragraph 1.6 provides explicit obligation to ensure STR filing is not delayed;
- Paragraph 3.1 states the purpose of AML/CFT transaction monitoring is to detect potentially suspicious and unusual transactions in a timely manner to ensure that STRs are promptly submitted to the FIU, once suspicion of ML/TF is confirmed;
- Paragraph 4.3 provides explicit requirement on timeliness and documentation of STR by officers conducting reviews and alerts;
- Paragraph 6.2.3 provides requirements to include STR filing in independent audit scope.

column17:
- Paragraphs 1.1-1.3 requires FIs to establish controls and processes as part of AML/CFT internal controls requirements and maintaining systems as part of internal control framework.
- Paragraph 1.6 states the expectations for FIs to have in place written policies and procedures for the AML/CFT transaction monitoring programme implemented where it should be updated and should not delay the filing of STRs to the FIU;
- Explicit internal control requirement to prevent tipping off (paragraph 4.7);
- Requirements for FIs to have in place internal controls in regard to data access, confidentiality and system security (paragraph 4.8);
- FIs are expected to conduct quality assurance checks on a periodic basis on a sample of alerts to test the robustness and effectiveness of the identification and detection of suspicious transactions, parameters adopted, review and investigation of suspicious and unusual transaction and associated policies and procedures; detect and remediate any systemic weaknesses; and improve the quality of investigation of alerts generated and STRs filed (paragraphs 5.1 - 5.1.3)
- Governance oversight as a core internal control requirement (paragraph 6.4);
- Control escalation and governance accountability (paragraph 6.5).

column18:
-Paragraph 1.4: FIs are expected to adopt an AML/CFT transaction monitoring programme that operates on a risk-based approach and is best suited to the business model, size, complexity and risk exposure.
-Paragraph 1.5: Risk assessments and justifications for the AML/CFT transaction monitoring programme adopted should be conducted and documented in writing and provided to the Authority when requested;
- Paragraph 1.8.1: Explicit reference to assessing and understanding risk profiles.
- Paragraph 2.1: Requirement to identify and understand institutional and customer risks;
- Paragraph 2.4: requirement to assess and classify customers based on risk;
- Paragraph 4.4: requirement to reassess and update risk profiles on an ongoing basis;
- paragraph 6.2: Audit scope implicity includes assessment of risk management effectiveness within the programmes.

column8:
For individual customer and beneficial owner: full name; National Registration Identity Card (NRIC) number or passport number or reference number of any other official documents of the customer or beneficial owner; residential and mailing address; date of birth; nationality; occupation type; name of employer or nature of self-employment or nature of business;  contact number (home, office or mobile); and purpose of transaction.

For legal persons: name, legal form and proof of existence (e.g. Certificate of Incorporation/Constitution/ Partnership Agreement), unique identifier such as tax identification number or any other reliable references to verify the identity; powers that regulate and bind the customer such as directors' resolution, as well as the names of relevant persons having a Senior Management position; address of the registered office and, if different, a principal place of business; beneficial owners (includes directors/shareholders with equity interest of more than 25% or partners)

For legal arrangements: name, legal form and proof of existence (e.g., trust deed or equivalent document), unique identifier such as tax identification number or or any other reliable references to verify the identity; powers that regulate and bind the customer, as well as the names of relevant persons having a Senior Management position; address of the registered office and, if different, a principal place of business; beneficial owners [e.g., identity of the settlor, the trustee/s, the protector (if any), the beneficiary or class of beneficiaries and objects of a power, and any other natural person exercising ultimate effective control over the legal arrangement (including through the chain of control/ownership);

column19:
The Central Body shall coordinate with international and regional organizations and State parties of international or regional organizations relating to anti-money laundering to take appropriate measures including the following matters:
(a) exchanging information relating to money laundering and financing of terrorism;
(b) arranging, preparing, managing the anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism and cooperating in accord with the Mutual Legal Assistance in Criminal Matters Law;
(c) conducting training, technical cooperation and capacity building on anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism;
(d) conducting the awareness of anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism;
(e) cooperating capacity building in anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism compatible with international standards;
(f) cooperating in research and development functions of anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism.

- Party to key international conventions, including the Vienna Convention, Palermo Convention, and UNCAC
- Member of the Egmont Group (limited engagement) and Asia/Pacific Group on Money Laundering (APG)

column16:
Wire transfer transaction reports must at least contain the following information:
a) Information about ordering and beneficiary financial institutions, including transaction name of the institution or branch; head office address (or bank code for domestic wire transfers, SWIFT code for international wire transfers); country of receipt and remittance;
b) Information about individual customers participating in wire transfer transactions:  full name, date of birth; identity card number or citizen identity card number or personal identification number or passport number; entry visa number (if any); registered permanent residence address or other current address (if any); nationality (according to transaction documents);
c) Information about organizational customers participating in wire transfer transactions: full and abbreviated transaction name (if any); head office address; establishment license number or enterprise code or tax code; country of head office;
d) Information about the transaction: account number (if any); transaction amount; currency; amount converted into VND (if transaction currency is foreign currency); reason and purpose of the transaction; transaction date; transaction code or unique reference number if no account number is available; identifier of the originator sent by the ordering or intermediary financial institution to ensure traceability of the transaction;
dd) Other information as required by the Department of Anti-Money Laundering to serve AML state management in each period.

column19:
- Competent regulatory authorities shall, based on their range of functions and duties, conduct international cooperation regarding AML in: 
a) Identification and freezing of property or assets of money-laundering criminals;
b) Execution of mutual legal assistance requests;
c) Communication, provision and transfer of AML information with foreign competent authorities; 
d) Research, training, information support, technical assistance, financial aid and AML experience exchange;
dd) Other AML cooperation activities prescribed by law.

- Competent authorities of Vietnam may refuse to provide information or cooperate in cases where the request is inconsistent with the Constitution, laws of Vietnam, or international treaties to which the Socialist Republic of Vietnam is a contracting party, or where such provision may affect national security, sovereignty, or essential interests of Vietnam

- Party to the Vienna, Palermo and UNCAC conventions; member of APG
